{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  // The single Worker (ADR 18). Owns D1, R2 and every Durable Object class.
  // Credentials that authorize an external effect are Secrets Store bindings, read at the
  // point of use via `await env.NAME.get()` — never plain `env` properties (ADR 22).
  // Resource ids are deliberately absent: Cloudflare links them per-account, which is what
  // lets a customer's fork stay identical to upstream (ADR 24).
  "name": "mailda",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-01",
  "observability": { "enabled": true },
  "d1_databases": [{ "binding": "CATALOG", "migrations_dir": "migrations" }],
  "r2_buckets": [{ "binding": "EVIDENCE" }],

  // Outbound (ADR 23, ADR 33). The unrestricted form carries **no account-specific value**, so unlike
  // Secrets Store (ADR 28) it does not collide with ADR 24's byte-identical fork. `destination_address`,
  // `allowed_destination_addresses` and `allowed_sender_addresses` all take customer values and would
  // reintroduce exactly that problem — restriction belongs in the adapter and in D1, never here.
  "send_email": [{ "name": "EMAIL" }],
  // KEY_VAULT holds both root keys, generated per Node (ADR 28). No Secrets Store binding: a
  // `secrets_store_secrets` block needs an account-specific store_id in committed config, and
  // measurement showed removing it drops the binding silently rather than relinking the way D1 does.
  // A DO binding is declared by class name with no id, so this config stays byte-identical.
  "durable_objects": {
    "bindings": [
      { "name": "OUTBOX_SWEEPER", "class_name": "OutboxSweeper" },
      { "name": "KEY_VAULT", "class_name": "KeyVault" }
    ]
  },


  "migrations": [
    { "tag": "v1", "new_sqlite_classes": ["OutboxSweeper"] },
    { "tag": "v2", "new_sqlite_classes": ["KeyVault"] }
  ],

  // Delivery outcomes arrive on a queue (receipt: email-sending-events.md). A Node cannot receive its own
  // bounces — cf-bounce belongs to Cloudflare for the lifetime of the domain — so that queue is the only
  // channel by which "accepted" and "bounced" become observable per recipient.
  //
  // The **producer** binding is a provisioning lever, not something this Worker publishes to. Measured
  // (queue-provisioning.md): `wrangler deploy` provisions a queue named by a producer binding but fails
  // hard on one named only by a consumer. That much is unchanged.
  //
  // What changed (#72): the binding **names no queue**. A queue name is account-scoped, so the constant
  // that used to sit here made a *second* Node in one account unusable — its consumer registration failed
  // with "already has a consumer" and, worse, its producer binding attached to the **first** Node's queue,
  // so one Node's sending events were consumed by another Node's consumer across two D1 catalogs. Omitting
  // the name is measured to validate and build (19 August 2026, dry-run against a scratch config: the
  // binding table printed `env.SENDING_EVENTS  Queue` with no name), and it is *intended* to put the queue
  // in the same shape as D1 and R2 — provisioned per install, named after this Worker, which is per-install
  // by construction. Intended rather than measured, and the difference is the point: R2 in this shape is
  // measured (r2-auto-provisioning.md, `provisioning.r2_created_without_bucket_name`) while a nameless queue
  // producer is not. Cloudflare **documents** that provisioning now covers Queues and names the resource with
  // the Worker as a prefix; the exact derived string is **unmeasured**, so nothing in this repository may
  // hardcode or guess it. It is discovered from the deployed Worker instead.
  //
  // **The consumer cannot be declared here at all**, and that is a config-parse fact rather than a
  // preference: a consumers block with no `queue` field is refused outright — `"queues.consumers[0]" should
  // have a string "queue" field` (measured the same day) — and the only name it could carry is the derived
  // one this file does not know. `--var` and `--define` substitute into the *script*, not into config
  // values, so there is no interpolation route either.
  //
  // So the consumer is attached **out of band**, by `pnpm --filter @mailda/worker run queue:attach-consumer`
  // (`scripts/attach-queue-consumer.mjs`), which discovers the queue from this Worker's deployed
  // SENDING_EVENTS binding. That joins the step that already creates the `email.sending` **subscription** —
  // an account-level object, API-only, also absent from this file (queue-provisioning.md records
  // `queues.subscription_creatable_by_cli: 0`).
  //
  // **Accepted cost, stated rather than hidden:** a button-only install observes **no delivery outcomes**
  // until that step runs. doctor's `sending_events_consumer` reports the gap and `delivery_visibility`
  // reports the silence it causes, so neither reads as "nothing bounced".
  "queues": {
    "producers": [{ "binding": "SENDING_EVENTS" }]
  },

  // Browser-side scripts are served verbatim as text, so they stay real `.js` files that a
  // linter, a formatter and a person can all read. The alternative — JavaScript inside a
  // template literal in a `.ts` file — makes every backtick and `${` an escaping hazard, and
  // silently defeats every tool that would otherwise check it.
  // fallthrough keeps wrangler's default Text rules (.sql, .html, .txt) in effect; without it
  // this rule replaces them entirely.
  "rules": [{ "type": "Text", "globs": ["**/*.client.js"], "fallthrough": true }],

  // The authenticated application is React and therefore needs a build (ADR 30). This belongs to
  // wrangler rather than to `pnpm run deploy`, because a one-click install runs `npx wrangler deploy`
  // directly and never sees our scripts — measured in `deploy-button-install.md`. Declared here, every
  // route to a running Node builds it: the button, the CLI, and `wrangler dev`.
  //
  // Carries no account-specific value, so ADR 24's byte-identical fork is untouched.
  // `watch_dir` is the source tree only. The build writes to `generated/`, outside it — an artifact inside
  // the watched directory makes each build trigger the next, which is not theoretical: it looped
  // `wrangler dev` into unresponsiveness before this was pinned down.
  "build": { "command": "node scripts/build-client.mjs", "watch_dir": "src" },

  // The first-response clock's sweep (#41). One minute is cron's documented floor — the Minute field accepts
  // 0-59 and there is no sub-minute expression — so a breach is noticed within a minute plus an unmeasured
  // dispatch skew, which is nothing for a clock whose targets are stated in minutes or hours.
  //
  // Chosen over a Durable Object alarm because a DO clock's continued existence depends on its last run
  // having re-armed: stop re-arming and nothing external notices, ever. A cron schedule is not derived from
  // its last run. The cost is that cron documents no retry at all, which is only acceptable because the
  // sweep is a scan over due rows — see `response-clock.ts`.
  //
  // Carries no account-specific value, and needs no provisioning: `triggers.crons` lives entirely here, so
  // unlike Queues there is no producer-binding workaround for a customer's install to trip over.
  //
  // Changes take up to 15 minutes to propagate, so a freshly installed Node notices no breaches for that
  // long. `doctor` should report the capability rather than let silence read as "nothing is late".
  "triggers": { "crons": ["*/1 * * * *"] },

  "env": {
    // The environment the test suite runs in. It exists only because named environments do not
    // inherit bindings, and wrangler warns when the top level declares something this environment
    // does not — so the duplication below is drift-checked rather than silently divergent.
    //
    // It used to exist to *omit* CREDENTIAL_KEK, because a local Secrets Store binding cannot be
    // given a value from config. ADR 28 removed that binding entirely, so there is nothing left to
    // omit: the vault generates its own keys and works identically in tests and in production.
    "test": {
      "d1_databases": [{ "binding": "CATALOG", "migrations_dir": "migrations" }],
      "r2_buckets": [{ "binding": "EVIDENCE" }],

  // Outbound (ADR 23, ADR 33). The unrestricted form carries **no account-specific value**, so unlike
  // Secrets Store (ADR 28) it does not collide with ADR 24's byte-identical fork. `destination_address`,
  // `allowed_destination_addresses` and `allowed_sender_addresses` all take customer values and would
  // reintroduce exactly that problem — restriction belongs in the adapter and in D1, never here.
  "send_email": [{ "name": "EMAIL" }],
      "durable_objects": {
        "bindings": [
          { "name": "OUTBOX_SWEEPER", "class_name": "OutboxSweeper" },
          { "name": "KEY_VAULT", "class_name": "KeyVault" }
        ]
      },
      // No queue name and no consumers block, for the reasons given at the top level — and duplicated
      // rather than inherited because named environments inherit nothing. What holds this copy to the one
      // above is not the drift check, which compares only *which* binding blocks each scope declares: it is
      // the pair of tests in test/node/deployability.test.ts that walk every key path in this file, in every
      // environment, and fail on an account-scoped name or a consumers block wherever it appears.
      "queues": {
        "producers": [{ "binding": "SENDING_EVENTS" }]
      }
    }
  }
}
